{"object_id":"iteration_02","revision":1,"content_hash":"8943dd6f869be5193f5465c1237d297333bb743241c427f6e6f88a67c973d73e","source_run_id":"cloud-repair-formal-ar-03","input_refs":[],"unconsumed_metadata":{},"schema_version":"observation.v2","acquisition_id":"iteration_02","candidate_ref":null,"objective_ref":null,"sample_ref":null,"binding":{},"raw_blocks":[],"derived_features":{"legacy_projection":{"iteration_id":"iteration_02","route_id":"route_01","route_title":"Quarter-Wave Initialized Optimization and Order Verification","route_snapshot":{"route_id":"route_01","title":"Quarter-Wave Initialized Optimization and Order Verification","route_kind":"optimize_existing_stack","scientific_hypothesis":"The global minimum for mean reflectance in this constrained two-layer system occurs with the SiO2 layer adjacent to air and the TiO2 layer adjacent to glass, with thicknesses perturbed slightly from the ideal quarter-wave values (SiO2 ~99nm, TiO2 ~60nm) to balance the broadband response. The reverse order (TiO2/SiO2) cannot achieve a lower mean reflectance even with optimal thickness tuning due to the impedance mismatch profile at the air interface.","design_principle":"Impedance matching via graded index profile (Air -> Low Index -> High Index -> Glass) approximates a smoother transition than the reverse, reducing Fresnel reflections. Quarter-wave thicknesses provide zero reflectance at a specific design wavelength; optimizing around these values broadens the low-reflectance band across 450-700 nm.","proposed_materials":["SiO2 (n=1.46)","TiO2 (n=2.40)"],"proposed_topology":"Two distinct fixed topologies evaluated in parallel: (1) Air | SiO2 | TiO2 | Glass and (2) Air | TiO2 | SiO2 | Glass. Total finite layers: 2.","design_variables":["Thickness of Layer 1 (40-160 nm)","Thickness of Layer 2 (40-160 nm)","Stack ordering (discrete choice between the two defined topologies)"],"soft_objectives":["Minimize arithmetic mean power reflectance (450-700 nm)","Achieve mean reflectance < 0.03"],"manufacturing_considerations":["Thickness bounds strictly enforced between 40 nm and 160 nm","Lossless constant-index assumption maintained"],"evidence_ids":[],"theory_basis":["Quarter-wave condition: d = lambda_center / (4 * n). For lambda_center = 575 nm, d_SiO2 ≈ 98.5 nm, d_TiO2 ≈ 59.9 nm.","Fresnel equations dictate that placing the lower index material first reduces the initial air-interface reflection coefficient compared to placing the high index material first."],"expected_advantages":["Initialization near the physical optimum reduces the risk of the optimizer converging to a shallow local minimum far from the quarter-wave solution.","Explicitly testing the reverse order with equal optimization effort provides a definitive comparison rather than relying on potentially under-optimized baseline results from Round 1."],"known_risks":["The optimal solution might lie at the boundary of the thickness constraints (40 or 160 nm) if the quarter-wave condition is incompatible with the broadband requirement.","The discrete nature of the stack order means the optimizer must evaluate both topologies; if the search budget is split too thinly, one order might be under-explored."],"execution_request_english":"Execute a deterministic TMM optimization for a planar two-layer antireflection coating on a glass substrate (n=1.52) with air incidence (n=1.0). The stack must consist of exactly one SiO2 layer (n=1.46) and one TiO2 layer (n=2.40). Evaluate two fixed topologies: Topology A (Air|SiO2|TiO2|Glass) and Topology B (Air|TiO2|SiO2|Glass). For each topology, optimize the layer thicknesses within the range [40, 160] nm to minimize the arithmetic mean power reflectance for s-polarized light at normal incidence over the wavelength range 450-700 nm (5 nm steps). Initialize the optimizer with seed 17. For Topology A, include initial guess points near the quarter-wave thicknesses (SiO2=99 nm, TiO2=60 nm) and their immediate neighbors. For Topology B, use a uniform grid initialization. Allow a maximum of 64 solver evaluations total across both topologies. Report the best thickness pair and mean reflectance for each topology, and identify the global best candidate. Canonical user controls: incidence angles 0 degrees. polarization s. wavelength intervals 450-700 nm. soft objectives: minimize arithmetic mean power reflectance from 450 nm to 700 nm; achieve mean reflectance below 0.03 as a soft scoring preference. behaviors to suppress or avoid: high reflection in the 450 nm to 700 nm band. manufacturing constraints: layer thickness must be between 40 nm and 160 nm; exactly one layer of SiO2 and one layer of TiO2.","priority":1,"parent_route_id":"route_01","revision_reason":"Round 1 results showed a best score of 0.93 but lacked focused exploration around the theoretically optimal quarter-wave thicknesses. The feedback directive explicitly requests testing near SiO2 ~99nm and TiO2 ~60nm and confirming the superiority of the SiO2-first order. This route adjusts the initialization strategy to target these physical insights while maintaining the same hard constraints and objectives."},"compilation_status":"compiled","compilation_rationale":"The request specifies two fixed topologies with constant-index materials and explicit thickness bounds. I compile two separate optimization experiments, one for each order (Air|SiO2|TiO2|Glass and Air|TiO2|SiO2|Glass), to compare them as requested. Each experiment optimizes the two layer thicknesses within [40, 160] nm to minimize mean reflectance over 450-700 nm at normal incidence s-polarization.","compilation_errors":[],"run_status":"completed","physically_valid_candidate_count":5,"best_target_score":0.9071286432206414,"best_robustness_score":0.35107902415663017,"selected_candidate_ids":["topology_a_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__01","topology_a_sio2_tio2_opt__gradient_thickness__05","topology_b_tio2_sio2_opt__baseline"],"failure_categories":[],"experiment_summaries":[{"experiment_id":"topology_